    "body": "A program that answers on the Tor control port can hang the node at shutdown and point its onion connections at another host.\n\nI now accept the cookie file named in the PROTOCOLINFO reply only if it is a regular file. A FIFO made `fopen()` block the torcontrol thread, and shutdown waits for that thread. On POSIX the file is opened with `O_NONBLOCK`, so a swap to a FIFO after the check does not block either.\n\nI also use the SOCKS listener from `GETINFO net/listeners/socks` only if it is on the control port's host. That is a loopback address if the control port is on loopback, and the `-torcontrol` host otherwise. Other listeners, including unix sockets, are ignored and a warning is logged. The program can no longer name another host.\n\nIf no listener qualifies, I keep the onion proxy set by `-proxy` instead of replacing it with `127.0.0.1:9050`. A remote Tor whose SOCKS listener is on the `-torcontrol` host keeps working. A remote Tor that listens for SOCKS on another address now needs `-onion`, or a `-proxy` that points to it.\n\nTwo cases stay open. A program on a loopback control port can still name a loopback listener of its own, and a regular file on a filesystem whose read never returns can still block shutdown.\n\nTests:\n- a unit test for the listener rule in `torcontrol_tests`;\n- a FIFO case in `util_tests`;\n- functional tests in `feature_torcontrol.py`, with the mock Tor server, for the cookie file and the listeners.\n\n`doc/tor.md` says that the control port is trusted, and a release note describes the change.\n",
